the planet screen lung almost a third of  the world's land surface is covered by  forests forests have huge ecological  value they protect biodiversity and  stabilize the climate they remove carbon  dioxide from the atmosphere trees absorb  the greenhouse gas and store the carbon  in there would at the same time they  release oxygen but since forests also  have an economic value some 13 million  hectares are lost each year the  equivalent of 40 football fields per  minute slash and burn practices release  carbon dioxide exploitation of Natural  Resources pays as does converting  forests into farmland the UN program red  plus has been working since 2007 to  reverse the trend by making preservation  of forests and not their destruction  financially viable the idea is to  provide financial compensation for  governments companies or farmers for  reforestation and to prevent destruction  but the problem is previous climate  summits failed to finalize an agreement  on funding and concrete implementation  of redd+ even defining a forest remains  controversial at least ten percent of an  area has to consist of tree tops  according to one interpretation this is  what it could look like or if sparsely  planted like this even the height of the  trees is debatable some say two meters  others insist on a minimum height of  five meters depending on the definition  smaller trees could then be easily  chopped even without UN agreement on red  plus further bilateral forest protection  measures continue to be taken like in  Germany with funding of more than 30  million euros local populations are  meant to benefit from the funds  sustainable forestry and projects such  as eco tourism are meant to improve  their income and at the same time  protect the forest
 

